From a1130ddded3189dba22ca083aafaf351fb832c9d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 07:21:22 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 11/88] dns/fakeip: answer from memory, save to the cache file
 in the background

The store asked the cache file for everything. A query for a known name and
every new connection read bbolt (about 650 ns, 10 allocations). Every new
name was a transaction of its own: about 35 kB written to the file for one
record, and the query waited for it (a bbolt batch, 10 ms, under the store
lock: at most about 100 new names per second). The allocation position
reached the file only on a clean stop, so after a crash, an OOM kill or
SIGKILL the store started the range over and gave addresses clients still
held to other names. The range was a ring walked blindly: once round, a new
name took the next address whether its name was in use or not. Records were
never removed, and names that differ only in case got different addresses.

The store now keeps the whole map in memory; the cache file is a plain table
of addresses, read once at start, that receives the changes afterwards:

- Queries and connections are answered from memory (about 60 ns, no
  allocation). Names are kept in lower case.
- A new name takes the next free address of the ring; addresses in use are
  stepped over.
- An address is in use while its name is queried or connections to it are
  opened. A record unused for 24 hours is removed. When a range holds more
  than a quarter of its addresses (65536 at most), the records unused
  longest go, except those used within the last hour; at twice that a new
  name takes the place of the ones unused longest, whatever their age.
- Changes are written by a background loop, all pending ones in one
  transaction and without a pause: what arrives while a save is written
  goes with the next one. A query waits for the file only when the saved
  reserve of the position is used up or the range is full.
- The allocation position is saved ahead of the addresses given out, 1024
  at a time. After a stop without a save allocation goes on behind that
  reserve and the free addresses inside it are held back for an hour, so
  an address given out just before a kill does not reach another name. A
  record leaves memory only after its removal is written: a crash cannot
  bring an old name back to an address that was given out again.
- The file keeps the address -> name bucket and the position in a bucket
  of its own; the name -> address buckets are dropped when the table is
  read. On a clean stop the position is also written where upstream reads
  it. A cache file recreated after damage gets the table again.
- "Flush FakeIP" of the Clash API clears the store, not only the file.

Costs. About 170 bytes of heap per name (5 MB at 30 000 names; the file is
2 MB smaller). A name given out in the last moments before a kill may have
no record: a connection to its address is refused, it never reaches another
name. Upstream sing-box on such a file gives known names new addresses once
(the file has no name index for it), and after an unclean stop of this
store it starts the range over, as it does after its own crash.

Lab (podkop on OpenWrt, x86). A new name costs 40-50 us of CPU instead of
460-680. Written to the cache file for one new name: 21 kB instead of
29-35 kB when names come one by one (50 a second); 5 kB when 3000 names
come as fast as they are answered and the file is in tmpfs; 0.3 kB when it
is on emulated SPI-NAND, where a save takes longer and more names share
it. A burst of 100 new names gets its last answer in 2-4 ms, on the
emulated flash too (upstream: about 1 s). kill -9 right after such a burst:
all 100 addresses still lead to their names. A range of 29 addresses and
100 new names: a name queried all the time keeps its address.
